---
title: Adding a new specimen record
sidebar:
  label: Adding records
  order: 2
---

{% steps %}
1. Open `Specimens` from the bottom navigation bar, or from the side rail on a
   wide screen.

2. Select the add icon in the app bar to create a specimen. On an empty list,
   select `Create specimen`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    If every specimen gets the same parts, open a specimen that has them and
    choose `Duplicate part` from the three-dot menu. NAHPU immediately creates
    a new specimen with a copy of each part's type, count, treatments, storage,
    and museum fields. Tissue IDs, barcodes, preparators, dates, and remarks are
    not copied; fill them in on the new specimen. Nothing is created if the
    specimen has no parts.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select `Cataloger`.

    This is the person responsible for cataloging the specimen. NAHPU also sets
    `Preparator` and `Determiner` to this person. Depending on the project's
    field ID mode, the field number comes from the cataloger or from the
    project.

4. Change `Preparator` if someone else prepared the voucher or part.

5. Select the specimen `Condition`.

    The default values are `Freshly euthanized`, `Good`, `Fair`, `Poor`,
    `Rotten`, `Released`, and `Unknown`. Edit the list in `Settings` >
    `Specimens` > `Conditions`.

6. Add the `Collection date`, `Collection time`, `Prep. date`, and `Prep. time`
   when available.

7. Under the identification fields, select the `Taxon`.

    Start typing to search the project's taxon registry, then select the
    matching record. NAHPU fills the `Taxon Info` panel from it. Because the
    registry holds records at any rank, material not yet identified to species
    can be linked to its family or genus and refined later.

8. Change `Determiner` if someone other than the cataloger identified it.

9. Select `ID Confidence`: `Low`, `Medium`, or `High`. It tells reviewers how
   sure the identification is and helps curators decide what to check. Once it
   is set, `Identification Method` appears.

{% /steps %}
